===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|non|Svalbarð|g=n}} probably through Norwegian. From {{compound|non|svalr|barð|t1=cool|t2=edge, brim}}, thus roughly meaning {{mention-gloss|cold shore}}, probably alluding to the treeless landscape mostly covered with grass, although there is uncertainty as to what the name originally referred to. Cognate with {{cog|fo|Svalbarð}} and {{cog|is|Svalbarði}}. The name was first attested in annales referring to year 1194 and using either nominative form {{m|non|Svalbarði}}, either genitive {{m|non|Svalbarðs}}, although the original form may be reconstructed as ''Svalbarð''. <ref>{{cite-book|year=2020|author=L. Chekin|title=Svalbarðs fundr : The Place Name Svalbard and Its Connotations in Medieval and Modern Literature and Cartography|doi=https://doi.org/10.7557/13.5025}}</ref>

Also known from early sources as {{m|non|Svalbarðr}} or in dative form {{m|non|Svalbarða}}.

The original meaning of the Old Norse word is uncertain, but, according to Nansen and Alexander Bugge, may be the same as the modern Svalbard archipelago. <ref>{{cite-book|year=1925|author=R. Hennig|title=Von rätselhaften Ländern : Versunkene Stätten der Geschichte}}</ref>

